WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.840
- I've gotten plenty of
'thank you, come again.'

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.153
- You know, it's not...

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.003
It's not real.

00:00:05.003 --> 00:00:07.770
- Probably a step back, probably
a step back for Indians.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.870
- I grew up in India, I am
Indian, and in this video,

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.850
we are going to be talking about
fictional Indian characters

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.870
in Western television and film.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.670
- The main gripe I have
is that really it's just

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.023
so underrepresented.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.913
- I think it's changed a
lot over the past few years.

00:00:26.023 --> 00:00:28.810
- We're not in the
perfect moment, you know,

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.563
but I think we're, you
know, we're coming along.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.143
- I was thinking of dressing
up as Indiana Jones'

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.827
mocha-skinned lovechild.
(laughing)

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:40.913
Indian Jones.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.660
- Alright, this is Raj Koothrappali

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.160
from The Big Bang Theory.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.280
- White writers, they
always make an Indian name

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.360
for the character that even
Indians can't pronounce.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.193
- Great character.

00:00:51.193 --> 00:00:53.390
I mean, I love Raj
Koothrappali from the show.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.473
I think he's written really well.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.280
- Probably the worst
character on TV right now

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.113
for brown people.

00:00:59.113 --> 00:01:01.610
- Raj kind of feels like a blend

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.730
of a lot of different stereotypes.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.830
- But as an Indian,

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:07.453
I sort of have a love hate
relationship with him.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.000
- Really intelligent, he has
a job in the field of science,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.600
he comes from a rich background.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.900
- Why does the Indian guy
have to be the biggest loser

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.530
out of all these four geeks?

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.870
- I love theater myself.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:19.510
My whole life's basically
one long Vagina Monologue.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:22.330
- So, this is Mindy
Lahiri from Mindy Project.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.520
Mindy Lahiri is a really
awesome character.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.380
Mindy Kaling really created a character

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.880
that we haven't seen before
with South Asian females.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.400
She's humorous, she's really ambitious.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.120
- I think it's a step forward
to having Indians represented

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.130
in leading roles, which
I think is very good.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.490
I don't know if the show
necessarily does the best job

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.420
of exploring her culture that often

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.820
given that she tries to ignore
it for most of the show.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.290
- Mindy Lahiri has had some criticism,

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:51.720
and I have some of my own criticism

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.513
as well about the character.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.640
One of the main things

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.730
is that she only dates hot white guys.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:58.720
- Oh, man, I was a little worried.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.500
Some of my friends have racist grandmas.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.740
- Oh, I see.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.520
You assumed I was a racist
just because I'm old.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.550
- Well, now the tables have turned.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:06.383
I'm sorry.

00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:10.130
- So, this is Dev Shah
from Master of None.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.650
This was a really
groundbreaking show, I think,

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.001
for the South Asian community.

00:02:14.001 --> 00:02:16.880
- Easily the most relatable character,

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.020
Indian character, on a TV show.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.220
He's just him, you know?

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.080
People view him for what he
is, not because he's Indian.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.020
- Dev Shah is a pretty
accurate representation of

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.883
what a lot of Indians would
experience in America today.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.300
- He talks a little bit more
about relationships and dating

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.633
and career stuff through
the South Asian lens.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:38.720
- There is, again,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.383
disproportionate amount of
white girls in this show.

00:02:41.383 --> 00:02:44.002
Still want a brown girl
here and there, you know?

00:02:44.002 --> 00:02:44.835
Just saying.

00:02:44.835 --> 00:02:46.500
Throw in a Basanti for every Britney.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.180
- You know, Ben Kingsley
did an accent in Gandhi

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.320
and he won the Oscar for it, so...

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.890
- But he didn't win the Oscar
just for doing the accent.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.633
I mean, it wasn't an Oscar
for Best Indian Accent.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.070
- Casting people would always

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.215
ask brown people to do an accent,

00:03:00.215 --> 00:03:01.796
and I think this episode

00:03:01.796 --> 00:03:04.345
made them feel a little bit bad about it.

00:03:04.345 --> 00:03:05.860
- You're getting money

00:03:06.051 --> 00:03:10.080
for a role that you might be
perpetuating the same ideas

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.270
about what a culture might be.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:16.140
- While I find that enough of
a turn on to get into this car

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.973
and do what we just did, in the long run,

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:20.633
definitely not my type.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.633
- This is the lovely Priyanka Chopra

00:03:22.633 --> 00:03:26.283
playing the role of Alex
Parrish from Quantico.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.770
- The fact that she's
playing this role is awesome.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.110
- Priyanka Chopra is actually...

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.640
Was born and raised in India.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.240
So, for her to be kind of this sex symbol

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.480
and kissing another person on screen

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.110
is actually a really big thing

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.380
in Bollywood and Indian community

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:43.210
where you really don't
see actors and actresses

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.860
having that kind of physical relationship.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:46.960
- That is interesting.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.240
That's not a thing that Indian
culture would've allowed,

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.869
but in this American show,
it's a very natural thing.

00:03:51.869 --> 00:03:56.280
- It's kind of refreshing to
see this big step be taken

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.710
in this particular scene.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.250
- Again, it's a white guy.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.900
The more I see these clips,
the more I'm realizing it.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.020
Yeah, I guess you can not have

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.280
a brown brown relationship on screen.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.300
Is that, like, not financially feasible?

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.180
I don't know.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.340
- Do you have any idea what it's like

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.680
knowing my real brother and mother

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.980
spend every day of their
lives looking for me?

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.488
- Yeah, this is Saroo from the movie Lion.

00:04:19.488 --> 00:04:21.540
- Saroo, I think, is a really interesting

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.510
person to talk about when
talking about representation

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.410
because he was adopted

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.740
and grew up in a mostly white community

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.680
wanting to find this identity.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.430
I really actually kind
of identified with Saroo.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:36.370
A lot of, I think, second
generation Indian people

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:38.670
really feel this balancing tightrope

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.520
where you're trying to
juggle the community,

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.307
like the American community,
but also your Indian community.

00:04:44.307 --> 00:04:47.020
- Hollywood loves casting
Indians as street boys.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:49.260
It's either you're an orphan or a doctor.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.560
It's just the highest
level of sort of a sector

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.210
or you have no parents.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.723
- I mean, they are
realities in our country,

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.880
but there is, of course,
a lot more to our country

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.163
than just the slums.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:00.143
- I'm gonna cry.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.720
What I also love about this scene

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.200
is seeing the entire village come together

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.310
to really exemplify
that sense of community.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.910
That is something that we
focus on so much in India.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:17.740
It's a small little detail,

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.470
but to see everyone kind of
crowd around this reunion,

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:21.973
it feels very real.

00:05:22.265 --> 00:05:25.003
- (sniffs) Jiminy Cricket.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:26.653
Expired ham.

00:05:28.317 --> 00:05:30.420
- Uh oh (laughs)

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.323
- Apu, apu, apu, apu.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.870
- This is Apu, not gonna
pronounce his last name,

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:35.920
from The Simpsons.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:37.730
- This guy is just...

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:41.943
Created such a bad impression
for Indians worldwide.

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.000
- Here's the thing.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.630
He was the only Indian
entity in my life on TV

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.893
until I was 13, 14.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.810
So, while I was growing up as a kid,

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.050
he's the only Indian I've ever seen.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.000
- The problem with Apu is just that

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:58.793
he has been the only character,
prominent Indian character,

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:01.803
on American television
for the longest time.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.430
So, if someone has never
met an Indian person before

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.170
and they've seen The Simpsons,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:10.383
you're gonna think I talk
like that, but I don't.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.830
- Everything that we call
out as a stereotype today

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.240
exists in Apu.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.210
- It is a case of ignorance
as opposed to malice.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.560
They just did not know
enough about the culture

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:21.843
to accurately portray it on screen.

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.830
- Obviously there are Indian people

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:26.770
that run convenience
stores and gas stations

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:28.723
and drive taxis.

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:33.240
We're not saying that that
doesn't exist, but again,

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.990
you're just showing one
side of their character,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.620
and you're making fun of it.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:41.620
- He is intended to be laughed at.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.436
His Indian-ness is something that

00:06:44.436 --> 00:06:47.440
is supposed to be laughed at
and supposed to be made fun of.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.233
So, in that sense, I would say
Apu is definitely offensive.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:01.453
- Jess, Bend It Like Beckham.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.160
Jess is a fuckin' badass.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:04.940
Stereotypes that you kind of generally see

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:08.110
about Indian women are very...

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.770
They're quiet, they're conservative,

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.883
they're very marriage obsessed,

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.053
and Jess isn't like any of them.

00:07:15.053 --> 00:07:17.803
- This is a very good
story with good characters.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.560
Maybe the mom was a little caricature.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.040
She's like 'get married now,'
maybe that was a little much.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.010
- She is a really cool character

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.843
because she wanted to play soccer,

00:07:24.843 --> 00:07:29.374
but she also wanted to please
and support her parents.

00:07:29.374 --> 00:07:32.060
- Some Indian girls and boys do go through

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.510
that sort of feeling where
they're stuck in between

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:37.823
trying to keep their parents happy,

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.820
but also they want to
find their individuality.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.063
- They did so much risk to get here.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:45.380
They want you to also be safe,

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.130
and they want you to be well-off.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:48.560
- A movie like this actually, I think,

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.340
has helped to broaden
our society back home.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.520
- We're in a much further
place even from 10 years ago.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.268
I think people like Mindy
Kaling, Aziz Ansari,

00:07:59.268 --> 00:08:02.440
these types of people really
helped push the envelope

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.129
and really create opportunities

00:08:04.129 --> 00:08:07.106
for more South Asian representation

00:08:07.106 --> 00:08:09.623
and kind of breaking away
from the stereotypes.

00:08:09.942 --> 00:08:12.525
(upbeat music)

00:08:19.523 --> 00:08:20.920
- Hey, Unsolved is on a new channel.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.630
And now your part.
- Subscribe here.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:23.633
- That was my part.

